From 0e3a091e2a98945a5c1990e3e572793b64c6b837 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 21:34:21 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: Show and send video in 1:1 Teams calls

teams_media now receives the far side's H264 into a gst-shm socket and
sends our camera (luneos-synergy-connectors herrie/teams-video). Wire
it to the Phone app the way the Signal connector does:

- The engine's video lines (VSHM, VPREVIEW, VTX, PEERVIDEO, VIDEOERR)
  go to the LS2 bridge, which keeps the call's video state
  (teams_call_video.c) and adds incomingVideo/outgoingVideo,
  incomingVideoState and videoURI/videoPreviewURI to callStateQuery.
  audiod is told "video":true while the call has video.
- changeMedia: outgoingVideo turns our camera on or off,
  incomingVideo shows or hides the far side's picture.
- The m=video answer follows the offer (RFC 3264): recvonly when the
  far side sends and our camera is off, sendrecv/sendonly with our
  video SSRC in a=x-ssrc-range when it is on. The offers of a voice
  call (the Android app's first offer has video a=recvonly, its
  renegotiations a=inactive) are still answered a=inactive, as the web
  client does. The engine is told VIDEO 1 once an answer lets us send,
  with the size and bitrate the offer's a=fmtp:107 max-fs/max-mbps/
  max-br allow: the Android app's offers allow 176x144 at 208 kbit/s.

Turning the camera on mid-call takes effect with the next offer that
lets us send: we make no offers of our own in a call, and how the
Teams clients ask each other for video in a 1:1 call is not known yet
(no capture of a video call). Calls we place stay voice only.

tests/test_call_video.c covers the state, the engine lines, the JSON
and the SDP direction rules (GLib only).

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending

